detailedExplanation.whatItIs:
CSS is a styling language made up of rules. Each rule has a selector that targets some HTML, and a block of declarations that set properties. A simple rule looks like:
p {
  color: blue;
  font-size: 16px;
}
This says: every paragraph should have blue text at 16 pixels.

box-sizing is a property with two main values. The default, content-box, means width applies only to the content. border-box means width includes content plus padding plus border. A common global reset is:
*, *::before, *::after {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

Color values describe what color a property like color, background-color, or border-color should be. Examples:
color: red;
color: #3366ff;
color: rgb(51, 102, 255);
color: rgba(51, 102, 255, 0.5);
color: hsl(225, 100%, 60%);
Each line can describe the same or similar color in a different notation.

Pseudo-classes describe states or structural positions you can't easily express with a normal selector. Examples:
a:hover { color: red; }
input:focus { border-color: blue; }
li:first-child { font-weight: bold; }
tr:nth-child(even) { background: #f0f0f0; }
Each targets elements meeting a dynamic or positional condition.

!important is an annotation appended to a CSS declaration that boosts its weight in the cascade. Example:
.title {
  color: blue !important;
}
This makes the blue color extremely hard to override by ordinary rules.

A comment is text in your CSS that the browser ignores. The only syntax is /* ... */. Example:
/* Header styles */
.header {
  background: #fff; /* white background */
}
The slashes-and-asterisks wrap any note you want to leave.
